  html: `
<p><strong>Nothing else on this list came close.</strong> If you are cold where you sit, stand or work, the <a href="${HV_LINK}" rel="sponsored nofollow noopener" target="_blank"><strong>Solana Gear Heated Vest</strong></a> is the one I would buy — and it was not a close call. Here is exactly why.</p>

<h3 id="how-we-ranked">How we ranked the five</h3>
<p>We put the five side by side and scored them on five things that decide whether a heated vest actually keeps you warm: <strong>where the heat lands</strong> (how many pads, and whether any of them sit on the lower back and neck), <strong>honest runtime</strong> (hours per setting, not the “up to” number), <strong>what it costs to keep running</strong> (a universal USB bank, or a proprietary pack you must buy again), <strong>bulk and arm freedom</strong> (does it wear under a shell, and does the battery dig in when you sit), and <strong>all-in price</strong> (the vest plus the battery you need to use it). We went through each brand's published zone map, battery spec and return policy word for word, and read the owner reviews for every vest, looking for the complaints that repeat: cold spots, dead batteries, runs small, bulky.</p>
<p>Only one vest led on the first three, and it is also the cheapest on the list all-in.</p>
${TESTING_NOTES}

<h3 id="layers">Why more layers stopped working</h3>
<p>A sweater, a fleece, a blanket — none of them make heat. They slow the escape of heat your body is already producing. If you are sitting still, or your body simply runs cold, there is not much heat for the layers to trap, and sweat soaking into them pulls what there is out faster. That is why a fourth layer does less than the third did.</p>
<div class="callout"><strong>You have already run this experiment.</strong> If you have been cold indoors at 71°F under three layers, the layers were not failing. There was nothing for them to hold. What was missing was a source of heat, not another blanket.</div>
<p>A heated vest is the opposite kind of thing: a small, steady source of warmth against your core, so the layers you already own finally have something to trap.</p>

<h3 id="zones">The cold spot nobody heats</h3>
<p>Fabric barely moves heat sideways. A pad on your chest warms your chest and almost nothing else. So a heated vest only warms the places it has pads, and the gaps between pads stay cold no matter how high you turn it up. Coverage is about placement, not the number printed on the box.</p>
<p>Now look at where you actually go cold. The lower back you press into a seat, a tree or a bleacher. The kidneys. The back of the neck where the wind gets in. Then pull up any rival's spec page and count where its pads sit. Ororo's Classic vest publishes four zones: the two hand pockets, the mid-back and the collar. iHood's put heat in the collar, mid-back and chest. Neither has an element on the lower back.</p>
<figure>
