  <figcaption>The Solana Gear vest's nine pads: two at the front pockets, six across the upper, mid and lower back, one at the collar. Drawn from Solana Gear's published zone diagram.</figcaption>
</figure>
<p>The <a href="${HV_LINK}" rel="sponsored nofollow noopener" target="_blank"><strong>Solana Gear Heated Vest</strong></a> runs nine pads: two at the front pockets, six down the back in three rows — upper, mid and, yes, lower — and one at the collar. It is the only vest on this list with heat on the lower back, and you can check that against every other brand's zone map in about a minute without taking our word for it. That is the whole reason it ranks first.</p>

<h3 id="specs">What 9 zones and 8 hours actually buy you</h3>
<p>Nine carbon-fibre pads across the front, back and neck, three heat levels from one chest button (red high, white medium, blue low), and up to <strong>8 hours on the low setting</strong> from a 10,000mAh bank. The brand does not publish medium and high hours, and be suspicious of any vest that quotes a single number: on every heated vest ever sold, the headline runtime is the low setting. The elements are carbon fibre, which flexes where the old wire heaters used to crack, and the power connection is sealed and waterproof — which is the thing that actually decides whether a heated garment survives the washing machine. Water- and wind-resistant shell, machine washable, unisex XS to XXXL, rated by the brand for −40°C to 5°C, and TSA-friendly if you fly with it. The brand describes the elements as NASA-based heating technology; what matters to you is where the pads sit.</p>
<figure>
  <img src="/images/hv-bank.webp" alt="A flat 10,000mAh USB power bank plugged into the vest's inner zip pocket" width="758" height="496" loading="lazy" decoding="async" />
  <figcaption>The power sits flat in an inner zip pocket — a slim bank, not a cylinder in your ribs. Solana Gear image.</figcaption>
</figure>

<h3 id="reflex">Why your hands go cold before anything else</h3>
<p>When your core cools, your body protects its organs by narrowing the blood vessels to your hands and feet. Cold fingers are a signal that your core is under-heated, not a problem with your fingers. Heating your hands directly fights that reflex; warming your torso releases it. In one cold-physiology study, warming the torso kept finger blood flow nearly eight times higher than warming the hands themselves, at the same finger temperature.</p>
<p>That is why the first heated garment most people buy is a vest rather than gloves, and why the collar pad matters more than it sounds: the neck is where a lot of that core heat leaves. This is ordinary physiology, not a medical claim — if you have a circulation condition, talk to your doctor about what is right for you.</p>
${CTA}

<figure class="narrow">
